# Insight into Ion Transfer through the Sub-Nanometer Channels in Zeolitic Imidazolate Frameworks

## abstract
A crack-free sub-nanometer composite structure for the study of ion transfer was constructed by in situ growth of ZIF-90 [Zn(ICA)2 , ICA=Imidazole-2-carboxaldehyde] on the tip of a glass nanopipette. The potential-driven ion transfer through the sub-nanometer channels in ZIF-90 is strongly influenced by the pH of the solution. A rectification ratio over 500 is observed in 1 m KCl solution under alkaline conditions (pH 11.58), which is the highest value reported under such a high salt concentration. Fluorescence experiments show the super-high rectification ratio under alkaline conditions results from the strong electrostatic interaction between ions and the sub-nanometer channels of ZIF-90. In addition to providing a general pathway for further study of mass-transfer process through sub-nanometer channels, the approach enable all kinds of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) to be used as ionic permselectivity materials in nanopore-based analysis.

The studies on mass transfer through nanopores or nanochannels can greatly help us understand the fundamentals of important processes, such as DNA sequencing and ion transmembrane transport and permselectivity.[1,2] Various nanopores/nanochannels including protein nanopores[3,4] and solid state nanopores[5] have been used in mass-transfer studies. When the aperture size decreases from micrometer to a few nanometers, the interaction between transmission particles and the inner wall of the channel gradually dominates the mass-transfer process.[6,7] For channels with apertures less than $1\mathrm{nm}$ , though many studies have dealt with the use of such materials in gas separation,[8] few studies dealt with the process of ion or molecular transfer and permselectivity through such sub-nanometer channels.[9]
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) and their sub-class zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) are compounds formed by the coordination of metal ions with organic ligands displaying sub-nanometer sized porous structures.[10] In 2013, Shieh and co-workers reported that sodalite topology, ZIF-90, could be synthesized in aqueous phase under moderate conditions.[11] The topology structure of ZIF-90 has a pore aperture of $3.5\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ (six-membered ring) and a typical pore cavity of $11.2\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ which are orderly arranged into sub-nanometer channel array.[12] Since their discovery, ZIFs have attracted intense attention in gas storage,[13] molecular separation,[10] catalysis[14] and micro-extraction.[15] Most applications of ZIFs are based on the molecule–ZIF interaction within the sub-nanometer channels. An in-depth understanding of mass transfer and permselectivity through the subnanometer channels will greatly promote their future applications. To perform studies in sub-nanometer channels, two conditions are required. Firstly, a proper means is needed to isolate solutions at both ends of the channels effectively. Secondly, any nanometer-scale cracks on the channels or leakage between ZIF and sealing material should be avoided as they will ruin the subsequent measurement. Recently, Marken and co-workers directly grew ZIF-8 [Zn(2-methylimidazole)2] “plug” into a $20\upmu\mathrm{m}$ diameter pore in a thin polyethylene-terephthalate film.[16] Rectification similar to that in materials with regular nanochannels was observed although the charge-transporting mechanism was still unclear.
Herein, the composite structure of ZIF-90 was constructed in situ at the tip of glass nanopipette with an aperture of $200\mathrm{nm}$ . Therefore, the solutions inside and outside the asprepared glass nanopipette supported ZIF-90 (GNS-ZIF-90) were partition off and mass transfer can only occur through the sub-nanometer channels within the ZIF-90 crystal. The in situ synthesis strategy and the small aperture of the glass pipette ensured a tight connection between the ZIF-90 crystal and glass. The potential-driven ion transfer through the subnanometer channels in ZIF-90 was found to be much different from that reported in nanometer-sized channels. The variation of surface charge on ZIF-90 with $\mathrm{pH}$ showed a much greater influence on the ion transfer process. Meanwhile, ionic permselectivity caused by the strong size-exclusion effect was observed in the sub-nanometer channels.
The in situ synthesis of GNS-ZIF-90 is shown schematically in Figure 1 a, where the zinc acetate (0.1m, $10\upmu\mathrm{L})$ backfilled glass nanopipette $200\mathrm{nm}$ orifice, see the Supporting Information, Figure S1) was directly immersed into ICA solution $(0.4\mathrm{M},1.5\mathrm{mL}$ ). The temperature of the ICA solution was kept at ${60}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to keep the ICA soluble. The two solutions met at the glass tip and formed crystalline ZIF-90, which gradually grew and blocked the pipette orifice. The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image of the fabricated GNSZIF-90 is shown in Figure 1 b. The crystalline ZIF-90 is bigger than the glass tip and tightly covered the whole tip. The size of ZIF-90 crystal can be controlled within $1{-}200\upmu\mathrm{m}$ by adjusting the reaction time (Figure S2). We presume that the zinc ions can diffuse through the ZIF-90, ensuring the continuous growth of ZIF crystal. After $13\mathrm{h}$ , the GNS-ZIF-90 crystal was
$1{-}2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ in size, which was used for subsequent experiments. The success rate of fabricating GNS-ZIF-90 was about $100\%$ . The prevention of any air bubbles at the tip of the glass nanopipette was the key step to get a fine structure. Because only the part covering the pipette orifice provides nanochannels, the slight variations of the crystal size and morphology in different GNS-ZIF-90s show no effects on the subsequent electrochemical and fluorescence measurements.
The ZIF-90 crystal synthesized on the tip of the glass nanopipette was characterized in situ by confocal Raman microscopy, and compared with the powder directly synthesized in bulk solution. As shown in Figure 1 c, the ZIF-90 at the glass tip exhibited the same Raman peaks as the ZIF-90 powder, and their Raman peaks could be assigned to typical vibrations of different groups in ZIF-90 as listed in Table S1. Infrared spectrum, X-ray diffraction spectrum (XRD), and SEM images (Figure S3–S5) also confirmed that the ZIF-90 powder had the same sub-nanometer channel structure with bulk-synthesized ZIF-90.[12]
Ion transfer through sub-nanometer channels in ZIF-90 was studied by recording the ionic current during potential scan. Similar to rectification phenomena in diodes, an asymmetric current–potential $(I{-}V)$ curve appeared when ions passed through the sub-nanometer channels, which could be attributed to the asymmetric migration of ions. The ionic rectification was first observed by Bards group in a glass nanopipette with the pipette orifice of a few tens of nanometer. Various studies have uncovered that the ionic rectification strongly depends on the surface charge density and the overlap of electric double layers inside the nanochannels.[17–19] To study the behavior of ion transfer through GNS-ZIF-90, the setup shown in Figure S6 was used. The solutions inside and outside of the nanopipette were kept the same if not otherwise specified. Two $\mathbf{Ag/AgCl}$ wires were used as electrodes and the bias voltage applied was defined as positive when the potential of the electrode inside the glass nanopipette was higher than the outside one. Normally, the ionic rectification phenomenon in nanochannels dies down when the electrolyte concentration is higher than $0.1\ensuremath{\mathrm{\mathbf{u}}}$ because of the attenuation of the electrical double layer.[17]
However, for the sub-nanometer channels in GNS-ZIF-90, a clear asymmetric $I{-}V$ curve was obtained even in $1\mathrm{{MKCl}}$ via adjusting the solution $\mathrm{pH}$ using KOH. As shown in Figure 2a, linear and slightly bent $I{-}V$ curves were observed in 1m KCl solutions with the increase of $\mathrm{pH}$ from 6.48 to 8.48. When the solution $\mathrm{pH}$ was increased to 11.58, a clear ionic rectification could be observed with the current at positive potential direction dropped to lower than $0.5\mathrm{nA}$ while the current at negative potential direction increased to more than $-200\mathrm{nA}$ . It is also of note that such a rectification behavior can be maintained within a wide potential range without breakingdown. As shown in Figure $2\ensuremath{\mathrm{b}}$ , the GNS-ZIF-90 showed a stable ionic rectification when scanned from $+5$ to $-5\mathrm{V}.$ Even wider potential ranges could not be tested due to the limit of the instrument. In any case, ZIF-90 crystal exhibited a good rectification similar to semiconductor diodes along with unique benefits of small size (around $1\upmu\mathrm{m})$ , low power consumption, and applicability in solution environment, which may enable it to be used in the development of novel nano-devices. The diode switching time was measured using chronoamperometry. As shown in Figure S7, it takes about 0.1–0.2 s for the current to reach a relatively stable value no matter of the step from $+1\mathrm{V}$ to $-1\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ or reverse. Such a response shows a fast transfer and accumulation speed of $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ through/in ZIF-90, which will be discussed in the part of mechanism analysis.
The rectification ratio $(R_{\mathrm{f}})$ is a factor that reveals the difference of current between the high current direction (current-on) and low current direction (current-off). Herein, we define the $R_{\mathrm{f}}$ as the ratio of absolute current value recorded at $-0.8\mathrm{V}\left(i_{-}\right)$ to that recorded at $+0.8\mathrm{V}\left(i_{+}\right)$ , that is $R_{\mathrm{f}}=\mid i_{-}/i_{+}\mid$ . Table 1 shows $R_{\mathrm{f}}$ value measured in different solutions. Although the $R_{\mathrm{f}}$ is not very sensitive to $\mathrm{pH}$ in solutions with $\mathrm{pH}$ from 6.48 to 8.48, it abruptly increase to more than 500 in the solution of $1\mathrm{{M}}\mathrm{{KCl}}$ at $\mathrm{pH}11.58\$ . As far as we know, this is the highest rectification ratio reported under such high salt concentration. The dependence of current on solution $\mathrm{pH}$ shows that electrostatic interaction in the subnanometer channels is much stronger than that in ordinary nanochannels.
Because ZIF crystals have been reported to be chemically stable in aqueous solution with $\mathrm{pH}13^{[20,21]}$ and the addition of KOH does not obviously increase the ionic strength, the appearance of high $R_{\mathrm{f}}$ in alkaline solution could not be caused by the structural change of the sub-nanometer channel in ZIF-90 or the solution resistance. The abrupt change of surface charge on ZIF-90 and glass nanopipette is most likely to result in the high $R_{\mathrm{f}}$ in alkaline solution. We thus purpose a possible rectification mechanism for the sub-nanometer channels under neutral and alkaline conditions. As shown in Figure 2c, the cone shape glass nanopipette is negatively charged in neutral solution because the isoelectric point of glass is about 4. Although the Debye shielding distance between ions is usually less than $1\mathrm{nm}$ in $1\mathrm{{M}\ K C l}$ solution, the electric double layers (EDL) at the glass/solution interface still exist to balance the negative surface charge on the glass. Therefore, the density of cations $(\mathrm{K^{+}})$ in EDL is higher than that in bulk solution.[18] The zeta potentials of ZIF-90 powder are close to zero in neutral solution and very negative in strong alkaline solution as shows in Table S2.
In the neutral KCl solution, both $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ inside and outside of the glass nanopipette can transfer through the subnanometer channels in ZIF-90 under the electric-field driven force (as shown in upper and lower halves of Figure 2c). Both ${\bf K}^{+}$ and $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ions experienced a dehydration–hydration– dehydration process because the hydration radii of $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ and $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ions are bigger than the size of ZIF-90 cavity (detailed discussion in the supporting information under Figure S8). The distributions of electric potential energy along the nanochannel are shown in red curves in Figure 2c. Therefore, the whole system behaves like a normal resistor and shows no ionic rectification.
With the increase of $\mathrm{pH}$ , the zeta potential of ZIF-90 becomes more negative indicating more negative charges are loaded on the ZIF-90 crystal. At the same time, there were more negative charges on the glass owing to the deprotonation process of glass in alkaline solution. The negative charges on both glass nanopipette and ZIF-90 electrostatically repulsed anion into the sub-nanometer channels in ZIF-90. Depending on the pH, $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ ions have to take on more and more duty to transfer charge through the negatively charged subnanometer channels. The charge and ion distributions in alkaline solution are schematically presented in Figure 2d. When a positive bias voltage was applied onto the nanopipette (upper half of Figure 2 d), $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ ions are pushed out of the glass nanopipette. $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ions can transfer to ZIF-90 very fast along the EDL of the cone-shaped glass because of the high density of $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ions and low resistance in EDL. However, $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ ions leave from ZIF-90 to the outside solution at a relatively slow speed because there is no EDL on the outside interface of the glass to accelerate the leaving of $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ ions from the subnanochannels to the bulk solution. Therefore, $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ ions accumulate in ZIF-90 and generate an energy barrier (red curve in Figure 2d) which slowed down the overall chargetransfer speed (current-off). The height of the energy barrier depends on the surface charge, which is a function of $\mathrm{\pH}$ . Because the sub-nanometer channels only allow one ion to pass through at a time and the free space has been occupied by the accumulated $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ ions inside these channels, no $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ ions can pass through the sub-nanometer channels although the accumulation of $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ ions attracts anions. When a negative bias voltage is applied inside the nanopipette (lower half of Figure 2d), $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ ions are pulled into ZIF-90 from the bulk solution. Due to the close contact of ZIF-90 with the glass, $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ ions pass through the ZIF-90 and can rapidly enter the inner solution of the glass nanopipette along the EDL on the glass. Therefore, no energy barrier formed (red curve Figure 2d) and the current increased linearly with the potential (currenton). Therefore, the total ionic current at the negative potential direction is higher than that of the positive one. The proposed rectification mechanism showed the strong effect of $\mathrm{pH}$ and surface charge on ion transfer through subnanometer channels. When GNA-ZIF-90 was put in solutions with different pH (inside and outside of the nanopipette) $I{-}V$ curves also show the corresponding changes, which validates the proposed mechanism (Figure S8).
Interestingly, the current turning point (the potential where current-on change to current-off) clearly shifted to negative potential of about $-0.099\mathrm{V}$ in alkaline solution. Moreover, the current turning point greatly depends on the starting potential that was used. As shown in Figure $2\mathrm{e}$ , with the increase of the starting potential from $+1\mathrm{v}$ to $+5{\mathrm{V}}_{:}$ the turning point shifted from $-0.099$ to $-0.68\mathrm{V}.$ Further experiments showed that such shifts were independent of the time when the positive potential was applied (Figure S9). This observation supports the proposed mechanism that the accumulation of $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ ions in the sub-nanometer channel raised the energy barrier, and it suggests that the height of the energy barrier is a function of applied potential and $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ ions can stay in ZIF-90 for a long time once they have accumulated. When the scanning direction was changed from negative to positive potential, the current passed through the origin coordinate and showed a linear change up to $+0.09{\mathrm{V}}.$ Then the current suddenly dropped to almost zero (Figure 2 f). This phenomenon confirmed that no $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ ions accumulated in ZIF-90 when negative potentials were applied. The small “peak” in the red curve in Figure 2 f shows the initial stage for the formation of the energy barrier: With the increase of potential, more and more $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ ions in the glass nanopipette enter the sub-nanometer channels of ZIF90. When $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ ions enter and leave ZIF-90 at the same speed (ca. $0.09\mathrm{V}$ ), $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ ions accumulate in the sub-nanometer channels and result in a sudden drop of ionic current with the increase of potential. It of note that this initial stage of the formation of the energy barrier can hardly be seen when directly scanning from positive to negative potential (as in the situations of Figure 2a,b and e) because this current is submerged in the charging current which is not recorded. All the data in Figure 2 were measured from more than five different GNS-ZIF-90s and can be repeated on the same nanopipette 3–5 times. Although the current values at the current-on side vary according to the orifice size of different nanopipettes, the relative standard deviation of measurement with the same nanopipette is less than $3\%$ . Furthermore, different GNS-ZIF-90s all show the same tendency upon the same stimulation.
We also selected Rhodamine B (9-(2-Carboxyphenyl)- 3,6-bis(diethylamino)xanthylium chloride) as a model molecule to study its migration behavior through ZIF-90 nanochannels in the presence of large cations. Rhodamine B contains 4 aromatic rings and branch alkyl chains, which is larger than the apertures in ZIF-90. As shown in Figure 3a, a reversed $I{-}V$ curve $\left(R_{\mathrm{f}}<1\right)$ was observed when both inside and outside of the GNS-ZIF-90 nanochannels was filled with $10\mathrm{m}\mathrm{u}$ Rhodamine B (neutral solution). Two factors are considered responsible for the reversion of ionic rectification. On the one hand, Rhodamine B cations may be adsorbed on the glass surface and inner surface of the glass nanopipette becomes positively charged. On the other hand, because Rhodamine B cations cannot pass through the sub-nanochannels because of the size-exclusion effect, chloride ions have to act as the main migration ions in spite of electrostatic repulsion. The ionic permselectivity of ZIF-90 was further studied by observing the migration of Rhodamine B solution $(10\mathrm{mM})$ into GNS-ZIF-90 nanochannels backfilled with $10\mathrm{mM}$ KCl. A constant potential of $-0.8\mathrm{V}$ was then applied to force Rhodamine B cations migrate into and chloride anions migrate out of the nanopipettes. During the migration time of 10 hours, the ionic current measured in the unmodified glass pipette was much higher than that measured in GNS-ZIF-90 nanochannels (Figure 3b). Figure 3c,d show the optical and fluorescent images of the GNS-ZIF-90 nanochannels after 10 hours of electrical migration. As can be seen, Rhodamine B entered the unmodified glass nanopipette and resulted in strong red fluorescence but no fluorescence was observed in the ZIF-90 modified nanopipette. The above observation supported the hypothesis that the Rhodamine B cations could hardly pass through the nanochannels in ZIF-90 and also proved the ionic permselectivity of ZIF-90 nanostructure.
In summary, ZIF-90 can grow in situ at the tip of glass nanopipettes and form a combined nanopipette-sub-nanometer channel structure. Ion transfer in the sub-nanometer channels of ZIF-90 was insensitive to salt concentration but strongly dependent on the solution pH. Super-high ionic rectification was observed in $1\mathrm{{w}\ K C l}$ solution under alkaline conditions. The formation of such ionic rectification was attributed to the accumulation of ions in ZIF-90 as a result of the strong electrostatic repulsion in the sub-nanometer channels. The sub-nanometer channels also show a sizeexclusion effect which showed reversal of ionic current rectification. The combination of glass nanopipette with ZIF-90 provides a general and convenient way to study the migration behavior of ions through sub-nanometer channels, suggesting its potential application in investigating the transport of molecule/ion, gas, and catalytic process in confined spaces.
## Experimental Section
Zinc acetate dihydrate and imidazole-2-carboxaldehyde were purchased from Alfa Aesar. All chemicals were used without further purification.
ZIF-90 in water: ZIF-90 powders were synthesized in aqueous phase. Briefly, imidazole-2-carboxaldehyde $\mathrm{(4mmol)}$ was added to deionized (DI) water $(10\mathrm{mL})$ in round bottom flask $(25\mathrm{mL})$ . To obtain a clear solution, the solution was stirred at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ . Zinc acetate dihydrate (1 mmol) was dissolved to DI water $\mathrm{10~mL})$ . Then, these two solutions were mixed and heated at ${60^{\circ}}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . The precipitate was immediately collected by filtration using filter membrane with $0.22\upmu\mathrm{m}$ pores and washed with ethanol for several times. Then, the resulting ZIF-90 powder was dried at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in an oven.
GNS-ZIF-90: Pipettes were fabricated using a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -laser-based pipette puller (P-2000, Sutter Instrument Co.) with glass capillaries (O.D. $1.0\mathrm{mm}$ and I.D. $0.58\mathrm{mm}^{\cdot}$ ) from the same company. Then, $10\upmu\mathrm{L}$ zinc acetate solution (0.1m) was injected into the glass nanopipette. Subsequently, the tip of the nanopipette was immersed into ICA solution $(0.4\mathbf{M})$ and kept at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $13\mathrm{h}$ to synthesize ZIF-90 crystal at the glass tip with diameter of $1{-}2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . Increase the incubation time result in larger ZIF-90 crystals.
